---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/Fracital/estrogen';
const UI_REPO =
	'https://github.com/javascript-obfuscator/javascript-obfuscator-ui';
---

<Base>
	<div class="ui container grid middle aligned stackable">
		<div class="row">
			<div class="column twelve wide">
				<div class="ui basic segment">
					<div class="title">
						<h1>estrogen obfuscator</h1>
					</div>
					<p>
						<a href={REPO} target="_new">estrogen</a> rewrites built JavaScript
						so a reader pays more to make sense of it. It is not secrecy: a
						decoder key ships in each file, right next to the data, and anyone
						with devtools can run the code back. This page runs the real engine,
						compiled to WebAssembly, entirely in your browser. Nothing is
						uploaded. Install it from npm as <code>@sysce/estrogen</code>.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="column four wide">
				<div class="ui basic segment">
					<svg
						id="Logo"
						viewBox="0 0 300 120"
						role="img"
						aria-label="estrogen"
						xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
					>
						<rect width="300" height="120" rx="12" fill="#fdf2f8"></rect>
						<circle cx="60" cy="60" r="30" fill="none" stroke="#d14b8f"
							stroke-width="7"></circle>
						<line x1="60" y1="90" x2="60" y2="112" stroke="#d14b8f"
							stroke-width="7"></line>
						<line x1="48" y1="101" x2="72" y2="101" stroke="#d14b8f"
							stroke-width="7"></line>
						<text x="108" y="72" font-family="monospace" font-size="34"
							font-weight="700" fill="#b23372">estrogen</text>
					</svg>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<div class="ui container grid">
		<div class="row">
			<div class="column">
				<div id="root">
					<div class="ui stackable grid">
						<div class="eleven wide column">
							<div class="ui top attached stackable three item tabular menu"
								id="tabMenu">
								<a class="item active" data-tab="code" href="#code"
									>Copy &amp; Paste JavaScript Code</a>
								<a class="item" data-tab="upload" href="#upload"
									>Upload JavaScript File</a>
								<a class="item" data-tab="output" href="#output">Output</a>
							</div>

							<div class="ui bottom attached tab segment active" data-pane="code">
								<textarea id="editor"></textarea>
								<div class="ui basic segment">
									<button class="ui primary button" id="obfuscateBtn"
										>Obfuscate</button>
								</div>
							</div>

							<div class="ui bottom attached tab segment" data-pane="upload">
								<div class="DropZone" id="dropZone">
									<div>
										Try dropping a .js file here, or click to choose one to
										upload.
									</div>
								</div>
								<input type="file" id="fileInput" accept=".js,text/javascript"
									hidden />
							</div>

							<div class="ui bottom attached tab segment" data-pane="output">
								<div class="ui stackable grid">
									<div class="sixteen wide column">
										<div class="ui form">
											<textarea id="output" rows="12" readonly></textarea>
										</div>
									</div>
									<div class="thirteen wide column">
										<button class="ui button" id="downloadBtn" disabled>
											<i class="download icon"></i> Download obfuscated code
										</button>
										<div class="editsCount" id="editsCount"></div>
									</div>
									<div class="three wide column middle aligned">
										<div class="ui checkbox">
											<input type="checkbox" id="evaluateChk" />
											<label for="evaluateChk">Evaluate</label>
										</div>
									</div>
									<div class="sixteen wide column">
										<div class="evaluatedCode" id="evaluatedCode" hidden></div>
									</div>
								</div>
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="five wide column">
							<div class="ui segment OptionsForm ui form">
								<div class="field">
									<label for="seed">Seed (optional)</label>
									<input type="text" id="seed" placeholder="leave empty for
										random keys" />
									<div class="infoPanel">
										<p>
											Empty means fresh random keys each run, so the output
											changes every time. A value makes the output
											deterministic: the same source and seed give the same
											bytes, so a build repeats.
										</p>
									</div>
								</div>

								<button class="ui button" id="sampleBtn" type="button"
									>Load sample</button>

								<div class="ui divider"></div>

								<div class="infoPanel">
									<h4>What this does</h4>
									<p>
										estrogen's app-chunk pass moves string and template literals
										into a per-file ChaCha20 pool and prepends the decoder.
										Identifiers and structure are left as-is in this browser
										demo.
									</p>
								</div>

								<div class="infoPanel">
									<h4>The limit</h4>
									<p>
										The decoder key ships in the output, right next to the data,
										and devtools can run it back. This raises the cost of reading
										a build. It is not secrecy.
									</p>
								</div>

								<div class="ui divider"></div>
								<div class="engineVersion" id="engineVersion">loading engine...</div>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="row">
			<div class="column">
				<h2 class="header" id="FAQ">FAQ</h2>

				<h4>Why would I obfuscate my JavaScript?</h4>
				<p>
					To raise the cost of reading a shipped build. estrogen removes the
					plain string and template literals from the source and puts them in an
					encrypted pool, so a reader can no longer grep for a message, a URL or
					a key name and land on the line that uses it. That slows down
					copy/paste reuse and casual inspection. It does not stop a determined
					reader.
				</p>

				<h4>Is this foolproof?</h4>
				<p>
					No. estrogen never claims secrecy. The browser has to run the code, so
					the decoder and its key ship in the same file as the data. Someone
					with the time and patience can run the decoder themselves and recover
					the strings. The point is cost, not a lock.
				</p>

				<h4>Why is the output larger than my source?</h4>
				<p>
					Because the pass prepends a decoder and stores the literals as an
					encrypted, base64 pool, which is bigger than the plain text it
					replaced. There is a lot of repetition in the decoder, so the output
					compresses well with gzip or brotli on your server.
				</p>

				<h4>Does it work on any JavaScript?</h4>
				<p>
					It works on standard JavaScript that parses. The browser demo runs one
					source string at a time through the app-chunk pass. If the input has
					no poolable string or template literal, the pass reports zero edits and
					the output matches the input.
				</p>

				<h4>Do you store my source?</h4>
				<p>
					No. There is no server. The engine is WebAssembly running in this page,
					so your code never leaves your browser.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="row">
			<div class="column">
				<div class="ui divider"></div>
				<div>
					<span>
						The interface is derived from
						<a href={UI_REPO}>javascript-obfuscator-ui</a> (BSD-2-Clause) by
						Timofey Kachalov and Tiago Serafim. The engine is
						<a href="https://github.com/Fracital/estrogen">estrogen</a>. See
						<a href="/THIRD-PARTY.md">third-party licenses</a>.
					</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<script>
		import '../scripts/app.ts';
	</script>
</Base>
